1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,051
One tent, high on a bare slope in the Ural mountains.

2
00:00:04,051 --> 00:00:06,156
February, nineteen fifty-nine.

3
00:00:06,156 --> 00:00:13,944
Nine experienced hikers cut their way out of it from the inside, and walked into minus thirty degrees in socks.

4
00:00:13,944 --> 00:00:17,733
More than sixty years later, we still argue about why.

5
00:00:18,199 --> 00:00:22,030
Some say the sky held glowing orange spheres that night.

6
00:00:22,030 --> 00:00:26,203
Some say a secret weapon, a yeti, or visitors from elsewhere.

7
00:00:26,203 --> 00:00:28,802
The Russian government says avalanche.

8
00:00:28,802 --> 00:00:31,333
Which of these survives the evidence?

9
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:40,075
Tonight we open the case file, and develop it one frame at a time, like the last roll of film the group ever shot.

10
00:00:40,075 --> 00:00:42,398
Facts first, then every suspect.

11
00:00:42,398 --> 00:00:44,866
This is the Dyatlov Pass incident.

12
00:00:45,333 --> 00:00:47,259
The Dyatlov Pass incident.

13
00:00:47,259 --> 00:00:49,037
Evidence against theory.

14
00:00:49,037 --> 00:00:51,333
Let us develop the first frame.

15
00:00:55,799 --> 00:00:58,016
January, nineteen fifty-nine.

16
00:00:58,016 --> 00:01:06,498
Ten students and graduates of the Ural Polytechnical Institute set out on a ski trek across the northern Urals.

17
00:01:06,498 --> 00:01:12,000
Their leader is Igor Dyatlov, twenty-three, a radio engineering student.

18
00:01:12,466 --> 00:01:14,059
Eight men and two women.

19
00:01:14,059 --> 00:01:17,510
All experienced, all trained for hard winter travel.

20
00:01:17,510 --> 00:01:27,133
The route would earn them the top hiking grade of the day, Grade Three, and the Sverdlovsk route commission approved it on the eighth of January.

21
00:01:27,599 --> 00:01:34,290
Their goal was Gora Otorten, a mountain about ten kilometers north of where they would camp.

22
00:01:34,290 --> 00:01:38,000
February is the hardest time to cross this country.

23
00:01:38,466 --> 00:01:45,654
They reach the town of Ivdel on the twenty-fifth, then ride a lorry to Vizhai, the last village on the map.

24
00:01:45,654 --> 00:01:51,766
On the twenty-seventh they ski north into the forest, and the last settlement falls behind.

25
00:01:52,233 --> 00:01:55,296
On the twenty-eighth, Yuri Yudin turns back.

26
00:01:55,296 --> 00:01:58,498
His joints hurt, and he has a heart condition.

27
00:01:58,498 --> 00:02:02,258
He shakes hands with his friends and walks back alone.

28
00:02:02,258 --> 00:02:05,599
He is the only member of the group who survives.

29
00:02:06,066 --> 00:02:06,874
Nine remain.

30
00:02:06,874 --> 00:02:08,489
Seven men and two women.

31
00:02:08,489 --> 00:02:16,900
They keep diaries and carry cameras, which is why we know their route almost to the hour, right up to the day before the end.

32
00:02:17,366 --> 00:02:21,164
Their diaries are the diaries of a tired, happy group.

33
00:02:21,164 --> 00:02:23,766
Nothing on those pages hints at fear.

34
00:02:24,233 --> 00:02:25,704
The first of February.

35
00:02:25,704 --> 00:02:33,258
They reach the edge of the highland, leave spare food and gear in the forest below, and start up toward the pass.

36
00:02:33,258 --> 00:02:36,000
The snow thickens and the view collapses.

37
00:02:36,466 --> 00:02:43,260
In the storm they drift west of their line, away from the pass and up the flank of Kholat Syakhl.

38
00:02:43,260 --> 00:02:48,233
In the Mansi language, the name is usually translated as Dead Mountain.

39
00:02:48,699 --> 00:02:50,449
They realize their mistake.

40
00:02:50,449 --> 00:02:55,570
The forest, with shelter and firewood, lies one and a half kilometers downhill.

41
00:02:55,570 --> 00:02:59,329
Instead they pitch the tent right there on the open slope.

42
00:02:59,329 --> 00:03:03,866
Yudin later guessed Dyatlov wanted to keep the height they had gained.

43
00:03:04,333 --> 00:03:09,222
To pitch a nine-person tent on a slope, they dig a shelf into the snow.

44
00:03:09,222 --> 00:03:10,462
Remember that cut.

45
00:03:10,462 --> 00:03:11,633
It matters later.

46
00:03:12,099 --> 00:03:16,246
Inside: nine sleeping bags, a stove, boots drying overhead.

47
00:03:16,246 --> 00:03:23,133
One of their last photographs, taken before sunset, shows them digging that very cut in the slope.

48
00:03:23,599 --> 00:03:28,160
The temperature falls toward minus thirty, and a storm builds.

49
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:35,664
The 1959 investigators arrived weeks later, in calmer weather, after fresh snow had covered the slope.

50
00:03:35,664 --> 00:03:38,533
They never saw it as it was that night.

51
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,826
Sometime after midnight, something happens.

52
00:03:41,826 --> 00:03:43,140
There is no witness.

53
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:48,266
All we have is a tent, nine trails of footprints, and a great deal of silence.

54
00:03:48,733 --> 00:03:53,136
Dyatlov had promised a telegram by the twelfth of February.

55
00:03:53,136 --> 00:03:57,092
A delay of a few days was normal, so nobody panicked.

56
00:03:57,092 --> 00:04:03,511
On the twentieth, relatives demand action, and the institute sends student volunteers.

57
00:04:03,511 --> 00:04:05,899
Soon soldiers and aircraft join.

58
00:04:06,366 --> 00:04:09,922
Search parties fan out across the northern Urals.

59
00:04:09,922 --> 00:04:15,800
On the twenty-sixth of February, a student named Mikhail Sharavin finds the tent.

60
00:04:16,266 --> 00:04:20,181
He describes it as half torn down and covered with snow.

61
00:04:20,181 --> 00:04:20,601
Empty.

62
00:04:20,601 --> 00:04:24,166
The group's belongings and boots were still inside.

63
00:04:24,633 --> 00:04:26,933
Investigators study the canvas.

64
00:04:26,933 --> 00:04:29,753
The tent was cut open from the inside.

65
00:04:29,753 --> 00:04:32,276
Not clawed, not torn from outside.

66
00:04:32,276 --> 00:04:35,466
Cut with a knife, by people who needed out.

67
00:04:35,933 --> 00:04:38,300
Nine sets of footprints lead away.

68
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:42,478
Some made by socks, one by a single shoe, some by bare feet.

69
00:04:42,478 --> 00:04:47,699
They head downhill, toward the forest edge, one and a half kilometers away.

70
00:04:48,166 --> 00:04:50,115
These tracks rule out a lot.

71
00:04:50,115 --> 00:04:53,734
They show nine people leaving on their own, on foot.

72
00:04:53,734 --> 00:04:55,335
No sign of anyone else.

73
00:04:55,335 --> 00:04:56,866
No sign of a struggle.

74
00:04:57,333 --> 00:05:02,023
After about five hundred meters, the tracks disappear under the snow.

75
00:05:02,023 --> 00:05:05,966
From there, the searchers have to follow the slope itself.

76
00:05:06,433 --> 00:05:11,807
That night it is about minus twenty-five to minus thirty, with a storm blowing.

77
00:05:11,807 --> 00:05:15,616
Later reviews suggest it may have fallen to minus forty.

78
00:05:15,616 --> 00:05:18,133
Dressed for sleeping, that is lethal.

79
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:20,209
So the question changes.

80
00:05:20,209 --> 00:05:22,557
It is no longer what attacked them.

81
00:05:22,557 --> 00:05:29,666
It is what frightened nine experienced hikers badly enough to leave warm shelter, in the dark, in a storm.

82
00:05:30,133 --> 00:05:33,752
At the forest edge stands a huge Siberian pine.

83
00:05:33,752 --> 00:05:36,833
Beneath it, the remains of a small fire.

84
00:05:37,300 --> 00:05:43,991
Here searchers find the first two, Yuri Krivonishenko and Yuri Doroshenko, in their underwear.

85
00:05:43,991 --> 00:05:51,466
The branches above them are broken as high as five meters, as if someone climbed up to look for the camp.

86
00:05:51,933 --> 00:05:58,451
Between the pine and the tent, three more: Igor Dyatlov, Zinaida Kolmogorova and Rustem Slobodin.

87
00:05:58,451 --> 00:06:04,566
At three hundred, six hundred and thirty, and four hundred and eighty meters from the tree.

88
00:06:04,566 --> 00:06:08,866
Their positions suggest they were climbing back toward the tent.

89
00:06:09,333 --> 00:06:15,445
The medical examination of these five finds no injury that could explain death.

90
00:06:15,445 --> 00:06:17,379
They died of hypothermia.

91
00:06:17,379 --> 00:06:21,866
Slobodin has a small crack in his skull, judged not fatal.

92
00:06:22,333 --> 00:06:23,554
That fire matters.

93
00:06:23,554 --> 00:06:25,860
It does not look like blind panic.

94
00:06:25,860 --> 00:06:33,933
It looks like people trying to survive: lighting a fire, breaking branches for fuel, sharing out what clothes they had.

95
00:06:34,399 --> 00:06:39,268
The most detailed reconstruction, from the Russian review, goes like this.

96
00:06:39,268 --> 00:06:42,031
Some stayed at the tree and kept the fire.

97
00:06:42,031 --> 00:06:45,451
Others tried to climb back for blankets and clothes.

98
00:06:45,451 --> 00:06:46,833
They did not make it.

99
00:06:47,300 --> 00:06:52,628
The autopsies add a detail: they died six to eight hours after their last meal.

100
00:06:52,628 --> 00:06:55,462
They were not starving, not lost for days.

101
00:06:55,462 --> 00:06:58,699
Something hit them suddenly, in the first night.

102
00:06:59,166 --> 00:07:00,131
Five are found.

103
00:07:00,131 --> 00:07:03,026
Four are still missing, and the snow is deep.

104
00:07:03,026 --> 00:07:05,600
Finding them takes more than two months.

105
00:07:06,066 --> 00:07:06,747
Five dead.

106
00:07:06,747 --> 00:07:07,632
Four missing.

107
00:07:07,632 --> 00:07:12,330
And a first working theory: a terrible winter accident, nothing more.

108
00:07:12,330 --> 00:07:15,666
The second half of the file will wreck that idea.

109
00:07:16,133 --> 00:07:17,469
Hold that thought.

110
00:07:17,469 --> 00:07:24,076
Mark what is solid so far: a tent cut open from inside, the hikers' own tracks, and cold.

111
00:07:24,076 --> 00:07:26,600
Nothing yet needs anything exotic.

112
00:07:27,066 --> 00:07:28,443
The fourth of May.

113
00:07:28,443 --> 00:07:35,172
Searchers probe a ravine seventy-five meters beyond the pine, under four meters of snow.

114
00:07:35,172 --> 00:07:43,966
They find the last four: Lyudmila Dubinina, Semyon Zolotaryov, Nikolai Thibeaux-Brignolles and Alexander Kolevatov.

115
00:07:44,433 --> 00:07:46,576
These four are better dressed.

116
00:07:46,576 --> 00:07:50,790
Clothing from the dead at the pine has been taken and worn.

117
00:07:50,790 --> 00:07:55,576
Dubinina wears a burned trouser leg that belonged to Krivonishenko.

118
00:07:55,576 --> 00:07:57,933
These were people trying to live.

119
00:07:58,399 --> 00:08:01,168
Then the autopsies change everything.

120
00:08:01,168 --> 00:08:04,386
Thibeaux-Brignolles has major skull damage.

121
00:08:04,386 --> 00:08:08,278
Dubinina and Zolotaryov have severe chest fractures.

122
00:08:08,278 --> 00:08:13,666
The forensic expert says the force needed was comparable to a car crash.

123
00:08:14,133 --> 00:08:17,952
Yet there were no wounds on the outside of their bodies.

124
00:08:17,952 --> 00:08:22,794
Injuries like that, with no marks outside, look like enormous pressure.

125
00:08:22,794 --> 00:08:26,000
That single detail launched a hundred theories.

126
00:08:26,466 --> 00:08:28,904
Some soft tissue is missing too.

127
00:08:28,904 --> 00:08:34,085
Dubinina's tongue and eyes, Zolotaryov's eyes, Kolevatov's eyebrows.

128
00:08:34,085 --> 00:08:39,876
The forensic expert judged this happened after death, in the running stream.

129
00:08:39,876 --> 00:08:43,000
Other researchers add scavenging animals.

130
00:08:43,466 --> 00:08:47,022
Some radiation is found on one victim's clothing.

131
00:08:47,022 --> 00:08:50,142
Relatives later describe orange-brown skin.

132
00:08:50,142 --> 00:08:51,811
Cold explains the skin.

133
00:08:51,811 --> 00:08:59,866
And the radiation was on only some items, not on all the group's gear, so it was not a blast that hit everyone.

134
00:09:00,333 --> 00:09:02,292
In May the inquest closes.

135
00:09:02,292 --> 00:09:05,908
Official conclusion: a compelling natural force.

136
00:09:05,908 --> 00:09:07,113
No guilty party.

137
00:09:07,113 --> 00:09:13,366
The files go to a secret archive, and the area is closed to hikers for three years.

138
00:09:13,833 --> 00:09:14,984
Now the suspects.

139
00:09:14,984 --> 00:09:18,233
Six theories, tried one by one against the file.

140
00:09:18,700 --> 00:09:22,265
Suspect one: an attack by the local Mansi people.

141
00:09:22,265 --> 00:09:26,122
Investigators questioned Mansi men and found nothing.

142
00:09:26,122 --> 00:09:34,200
Only the hikers' own footprints, no sign of a struggle, and the fatal injuries were too severe for human blows.

143
00:09:34,666 --> 00:09:38,386
Suspect two: animals, or a fight inside the group.

144
00:09:38,386 --> 00:09:40,394
There are no animal tracks.

145
00:09:40,394 --> 00:09:48,801
Researcher Donnie Eichar found a harmonious team, no drugs, and a small flask of medicinal alcohol, found intact.

146
00:09:48,801 --> 00:09:52,000
A brawl does not crush ribs without a mark.

147
00:09:52,466 --> 00:09:54,589
Suspect three: a secret weapon.

148
00:09:54,589 --> 00:09:58,835
Soviet parachute mines were tested in this region around then.

149
00:09:58,835 --> 00:10:05,341
They explode in the air and can cause internal injuries without outer wounds, as in the report.

150
00:10:05,341 --> 00:10:10,066
Intriguing, but nothing in the file shows a test happened that night.

151
00:10:10,533 --> 00:10:11,807
The orange lights.

152
00:10:11,807 --> 00:10:19,311
Another hiking group, about fifty kilometers south, saw orange spheres in the sky to the north that night.

153
00:10:19,311 --> 00:10:26,390
Similar lights were reported around Ivdel in February and March, even by military and weather staff.

154
00:10:26,390 --> 00:10:28,868
But none of it is in the 1959 file.

155
00:10:28,868 --> 00:10:31,133
The witnesses spoke years later.

156
00:10:31,600 --> 00:10:42,895
In nineteen ninety, lead investigator Lev Ivanov wrote that the inquest had no rational explanation, and that he was told to drop the reports of flying spheres.

157
00:10:42,895 --> 00:10:44,942
That is a powerful statement.

158
00:10:44,942 --> 00:10:50,166
It also comes thirty-one years late, and it describes lights, not a craft.

159
00:10:50,633 --> 00:10:52,230
Suspect four: aliens.

160
00:10:52,230 --> 00:10:54,663
A craft hovers, the hikers flee.

161
00:10:54,663 --> 00:11:02,876
Notice what that needs: a craft nobody photographed or recovered, and injuries that look nothing like burns.

162
00:11:02,876 --> 00:11:07,211
Lights in the 1959 Soviet sky could be rockets or flares.

163
00:11:07,211 --> 00:11:10,633
Nothing in the file puts a craft at the tent.

164
00:11:11,100 --> 00:11:12,820
Suspect five: infrasound.

165
00:11:12,820 --> 00:11:21,632
Eichar proposed that wind curling around the summit makes a vortex street, a pulse of infrasound that can cause dread and panic.

166
00:11:21,632 --> 00:11:23,559
It would explain the flight.

167
00:11:23,559 --> 00:11:29,066
But it is a hypothesis, not a measurement, and it does not explain the injuries.

168
00:11:29,533 --> 00:11:34,270
Suspect six: a katabatic wind, cold dense air pouring down the slope.

169
00:11:34,270 --> 00:11:47,933
A Swedish and Russian expedition in twenty nineteen said wind alone could have forced them out, and compared the place to Anaris Mountain in Sweden, where eight hikers died in nineteen seventy-eight.

170
00:11:48,399 --> 00:11:55,834
Paradoxical undressing, where freezing people feel burning heat and strip, may explain some of the missing clothing.

171
00:11:55,834 --> 00:12:02,500
But others in the group took extra clothes from the dead, which looks like clear thinking, not delirium.

172
00:12:02,966 --> 00:12:04,199
And the avalanche.

173
00:12:04,199 --> 00:12:15,024
In July twenty twenty, Russian prosecutors announced it as the official cause: a slab forced them out, and they made a hard, textbook retreat to safer ground.

174
00:12:15,024 --> 00:12:17,833
One official called it a heroic struggle.

175
00:12:18,299 --> 00:12:27,076
In twenty twenty-one, Swiss scientists Johan Gaume and Alexander Puzrin published a model in Communications Earth and Environment.

176
00:12:27,076 --> 00:12:37,000
A cut in the slope, a weak snow layer at about twenty-eight degrees, and hours of wind-blown snow can release a slab seven to thirteen hours later.

177
00:12:37,466 --> 00:12:42,958
Their simulated slab, about five meters long, strikes at two meters per second.

178
00:12:42,958 --> 00:12:51,647
Against a chest pinned to a tent floor, that gives moderate to severe, non-fatal rib injuries, just what the autopsies found.

179
00:12:51,647 --> 00:12:57,000
The authors admit it does not explain the radiation, or where the bodies lay.

180
00:12:57,466 --> 00:12:58,640
Critics push back.

181
00:12:58,640 --> 00:13:08,425
Searchers saw no clear avalanche debris, the slope averages only about twenty-three degrees, and the footprints look like people walking, not running.

182
00:13:08,425 --> 00:13:09,208
Fair points.

183
00:13:09,208 --> 00:13:18,666
The authors reply that a thin slab leaves little trace, and in twenty twenty-two they reported avalanche activity found by follow-up expeditions.

184
00:13:19,133 --> 00:13:20,619
Time to score the case.

185
00:13:20,619 --> 00:13:23,333
Evidence on one side, theory on the other.

186
00:13:23,799 --> 00:13:28,440
A tent cut from inside, hikers leaving on their own: evidence.

187
00:13:28,440 --> 00:13:31,210
Six of nine killed by cold: evidence.

188
00:13:31,210 --> 00:13:37,198
Chest and skull injuries with no wounds outside: evidence, now partly explained.

189
00:13:37,198 --> 00:13:40,866
A wind slab in a storm: the best fit, not proven.

190
00:13:41,333 --> 00:13:44,890
Weapon tests, infrasound, an attack: no proof.

191
00:13:44,890 --> 00:13:48,833
An alien craft: no craft, no wreckage, no evidence.

192
00:13:49,299 --> 00:13:50,213
The verdict.

193
00:13:50,213 --> 00:13:51,050
Not aliens.

194
00:13:51,050 --> 00:14:00,715
Most likely a sudden natural disaster at a badly placed camp, a storm and a snow slab, followed by a brave retreat in the dark.

195
00:14:00,715 --> 00:14:02,998
Not every detail is explained.

196
00:14:02,998 --> 00:14:05,433
But no detail needs a spaceship.

197
00:14:05,899 --> 00:14:07,130
Nine people died.

198
00:14:07,130 --> 00:14:10,965
Seven men and two women, aged twenty to thirty-eight.

199
00:14:10,965 --> 00:14:19,433
The best-supported story is also the saddest one: no villain, only a storm, a slope, and a decision made in the dark.

200
00:14:19,899 --> 00:14:29,665
Next case: a Bronze Age city in Jordan, where a team claims a cosmic airburst wiped everything out about thirty-six hundred years ago.

201
00:14:29,665 --> 00:14:33,382
Biblical Sodom, or a very bad day for a small town?

202
00:14:33,382 --> 00:14:35,933
Evidence against theory, next time.

203
00:14:36,399 --> 00:14:41,440
Which suspect do you trust: the avalanche, the wind, the weapon, or the lights?

204
00:14:41,440 --> 00:14:46,799
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
